# CASM WP39 trusted reference: casmordhaz1
#
# Independently hand-assembled from the casmordhaz1 fixture source in
# cmake/GenerateCasmTestFixtures.cmake (no .ORG, a bare instruction with a
# forward symbol operand as the very first statement). NOT produced by
# CASM. Proves the ordering-hazard fix (WP39): unlike casmnoorg1, which
# starts with a label, this source's first statement is JMP TARGET itself
# -- parserParseStatement evaluates TARGET's expression inline, before
# casmRunPass ever dispatches to emitInstruction, so this is the one shape
# WP38's four commit sites alone could not reach. Cross-check:
#   - brain/plans/2026-07-24-casm-phase8-wp39-relocation-classification.md
#
# WP41 -- updated with the R6 footer, staying deliberately byte-identical to
# casmnoorg1.ref.hex (same program bytes, same single relocation entry at
# offset 2). Cross-check:
#   - brain/plans/2026-07-25-casm-phase8-wp41-r6-footer-serialization.md
#
# Source (no .ORG):
#   - JMP TARGET
#   - TARGET: NOP
#
# Deliberately byte-identical to casmnoorg1.ref.hex: same addresses, same
# opcode, same default origin, same footer -- the point of this fixture is
# proving the *first-statement-is-a-bare-instruction* shape assembles
# correctly, not a different result.
#
# Hand assembly:
#   00 34        PRG load-address header ($3400, little-endian)
#   4C 03 34     JMP $3403           (forward reference, resolved to TARGET)
#   EA           NOP                 (at $3403, defines TARGET)
#   02 00        R6 table: one entry, offset 2 (JMP's ValHi byte)
#   00 34        R6 footer: base address ($3400, little-endian)
#   01 00        R6 footer: relocation entry count (1)
#   52 36        R6 footer: ASCII magic "R6"
#
# bytes: 14
# sha256: 77aca03cf9ec76e40246e0c273776937eccc18e03c5d9a36727c59635759ed2b

00 34
4C 03 34
EA
02 00
00 34
01 00
52 36
